### Quickstart



```
import Ravensight from 'ravensight';

const rs = new Ravensight({
  ingestKey: 'gt_live_your_publishable_key',
  gameVersion: '1.4.0',
});

// Reads the server kill switch and opens a session. Call it once at startup.
await rs.init();

rs.track('game_started');
rs.track('level_completed', { level: 3, deaths: 2, seconds: 91 });

// Optional: player feedback from an in-game form.
await rs.submitFeedback('The boss fight is too long', 'suggestion', 4);
```



In CommonJS, use `const Ravensight = require('ravensight');`. Events go out in batches on a timer and when the page is hidden, so you never have to call `flush()` yourself.

### Node servers with many players



The server fixes the device id when a session opens, so one client speaks for one player. On a game server, create a client per connected player, keyed on your own player id, and dispose of it when they leave. Node has no `localStorage`, so pass an explicit `MemoryStorage` to keep every client's id separate.



```
import Ravensight, { MemoryStorage } from 'ravensight';

const clients = new Map();

async function onConnect(player) {
  const rs = new Ravensight({
    ingestKey: process.env.RAVENSIGHT_KEY,
    platform: 'dedicated-server',
    deviceId: player.id,
    storage: new MemoryStorage(),
  });
  clients.set(player.id, rs);
  await rs.init();
}

async function onDisconnect(player) {
  const rs = clients.get(player.id);
  clients.delete(player.id);
  // Flushes what is queued, bounded by timeoutMs, then shuts down.
  if (rs) await rs.dispose();
}
```



Limits apply per game, so a burst of joins can hit a `429`; the SDK holds the events and retries.
